[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.308, "o", "\u001b[0;37m# Next, one trajectory: one attempt at one problem from DAPO-Math-17k (17,398 problems, each labeled with its answer).\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.006, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.006, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.004, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.259, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.31, "o", "\u001b[0;37m# A turn is one model call, ending at a tool call or an answer: one request to Dynamo.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.006, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.008, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.506, "o", "\u001b[H\u001b[2J\u001b[3J"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[1m🧮 One trajectory (Slime calls it a sample): sample 1481, 2 turns\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[1mprompt\u001b[0m   Solve the following math problem step by step. The last line of your response should be of the form\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         Answer: \\boxed{$Answer} where $Answer is the answer to the problem.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[1mHow many positive three-digit integers with a $5$ in the units place are divisible by $15$?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         Remember to put your answer on its own line after \"Answer:\".\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.005, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.01, "o", "  \u001b[1mturn 1\u001b[0m   … a number divisible by 15 must be divisible by both 3 and 5. The units digit is already 5, ensuring\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         divisibility by 5. For divisibility by 3, the sum of the digits (hundreds, tens, and units) must be a\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         multiple of 3. … I'll iterate through all possible values of h (1-9) and t (0-9), count those where (h + t)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         mod 3 equals 1. This can be efficiently calculated using Python code.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;33m<tool_call>\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;90m{\"name\": \"code_interpreter\", \"arguments\": {\"code\": \"count = 0\\nfor h in range(1, 10):\\n    for t in range(0…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;33m</tool_call>                                 \u001b[0;90m◀ generation stops at the tool call\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.013, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.011, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.509, "o", "  \u001b[0;32msandbox\u001b[0m  \u001b[0mcount\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m0\u001b[0m                                    \u001b[0;90m◀ ReTool runs the code in a Python subprocess\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;36mfor\u001b[0m \u001b[0mh\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36min\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34mrange\u001b[0;96m(\u001b[0;91m1\u001b[0;96m,\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m10\u001b[0;96m)\u001b[0;96m:\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0m    \u001b[0;36mfor\u001b[0m \u001b[0mt\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36min\u001b[0m \u001b[0;34mrange\u001b[0;96m(\u001b[0;91m0\u001b[0;96m,\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m10\u001b[0;96m)\u001b[0;96m:\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0m        \u001b[0;36mif\u001b[0m \u001b[0;96m(\u001b[0;94mh\u001b[0m \u001b[0;96m+\u001b[0m \u001b[0;94mt\u001b[0;96m)\u001b[0m \u001b[0;96m%\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m3\u001b[0m \u001b[0;36m=\u001b[0;36m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m1\u001b[0;96m:\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0m            \u001b[0mcount\u001b[0m \u001b[0;96m+\u001b[0;36m=\u001b[0m \u001b[0;91m1\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;34mprint\u001b[0;96m(\u001b[0;94mcount\u001b[0;96m)\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;33m<interpreter>                                \u001b[0;90m◀ the output is appended to the transcript,\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;32mOutput:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;32m30\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[0;33m</interpreter>                               \u001b[0;90m◀ and all of it goes back to the model: turn 2\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.008, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.012, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.508, "o", "  \u001b[1mturn 2\u001b[0m   The code confirms there are 30 valid combinations. Each combination corresponds to a three-digit number\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         (100h + 10t + 5) that meets both criteria. Therefore, the answer is 30.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         \u001b[1mAnswer: \\boxed{30}                           \u001b[0;90m◀ an answer ends the trajectory\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.012, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "  \u001b[1mreward\u001b[0m   \u001b[0;32m\u001b[1m+1\u001b[0m  reward_func: the answer, 30, matches the label, 30, which the model never saw\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.01, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.012, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0;91mwrong\u001b[0m    Another trajectory for the same problem ran  n = (a_n - a_1) // d + 1  with a_1 = 105, a_n = 990, d = 15:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.0, "o", "  \u001b[0m         all 60 three-digit multiples of 15, not just the 30 ending in 5. It answered \\boxed{60}: reward −1.05.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.012, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.012, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.01, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.51, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.31, "o", "\u001b[0;37m# Training makes what the +1 trajectory wrote more likely, and what the −1.05 one wrote less likely.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.009, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[1.013, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
